{"id":"e01t2","name":"Promo Code Settlement","lang":"pyspark","entry":"solve","params":["spark","rides_df","promos_df"],"limit_s":30,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>Marketing ran several promo campaigns last month. Each promo code gives a percentage discount on the fare, with a maximum discount per ride, and is valid only between two dates. The finance team needs to know how much each campaign cost.</p>\n<p>You are given two DataFrames.</p>\n<p><code>rides_df</code>:</p>\n<ul><li><code>ride_id</code> - the ID of the ride (string);</li><li><code>ride_date</code> - the date of the ride (date);</li><li><code>fare</code> - the fare before discount (double);</li><li><code>promo_code</code> - the code typed by the rider (string, can be null). Riders type codes in any case and sometimes with spaces.</li></ul>\n<p><code>promos_df</code>:</p>\n<ul><li><code>code</code> - the promo code, always upper-case (string);</li><li><code>pct</code> - the discount in percent (integer);</li><li><code>max_discount</code> - the maximum discount for one ride (double);</li><li><code>valid_from</code>, <code>valid_to</code> - the first and last day the code can be used (dates, both included).</li></ul>\n<p>A ride <b>uses</b> a promo if its trimmed, upper-cased <code>promo_code</code> equals a <code>code</code> and its <code>ride_date</code> is between <code>valid_from</code> and <code>valid_to</code>. The discount of such a ride is <code>fare * pct / 100</code>, but never more than <code>max_discount</code>. Rides with an unknown code or outside the dates get no discount.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return one row <b>per promo code</b>, including codes that were never used, with:</p>\n<ol><li><code>code</code>;</li><li><code>uses</code> - the number of rides that used the code;</li><li><code>total_discount</code> - the sum of discounts, rounded to 2 decimals (<code>0.0</code> if never used);</li><li><code>capped_rides</code> - the number of rides where the discount was limited by <code>max_discount</code> (the uncapped discount was strictly greater than <code>max_discount</code>).</li></ol>\n<p>Sort by <code>total_discount</code> descending, then <code>code</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p><code>rides_df</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>ride_id</th><th>ride_date</th><th>fare</th><th>promo_code</th></tr><tr><td>R1</td><td>2026-03-02</td><td>40.0</td><td>ramadan</td></tr><tr><td>R2</td><td>2026-03-10</td><td>80.0</td><td> RAMADAN </td></tr><tr><td>R3</td><td>2026-03-11</td><td>50.0</td><td>RAMADAN</td></tr><tr><td>R4</td><td>2026-03-05</td><td>20.0</td><td>Welcome</td></tr><tr><td>R5</td><td>2026-03-06</td><td>30.0</td><td>null</td></tr><tr><td>R6</td><td>2026-03-07</td><td>60.0</td><td>WELCOME10</td></tr></table></div><p><code>promos_df</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>code</th><th>pct</th><th>max_discount</th><th>valid_from</th><th>valid_to</th></tr><tr><td>RAMADAN</td><td>20</td><td>10.0</td><td>2026-03-01</td><td>2026-03-10</td></tr><tr><td>WELCOME</td><td>50</td><td>15.0</td><td>2026-03-01</td><td>2026-03-31</td></tr><tr><td>SPRING</td><td>10</td><td>5.0</td><td>2026-03-20</td><td>2026-03-31</td></tr></table></div><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>code</th><th>uses</th><th>total_discount</th><th>capped_rides</th></tr><tr><td>RAMADAN</td><td>2</td><td>18.0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>WELCOME</td><td>1</td><td>10.0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>SPRING</td><td>0</td><td>0.0</td><td>0</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 30 seconds (pyspark)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 2 GB<br><b>[input]</b> DataFrame rides_df<br><b>[input]</b> DataFrame promos_df<br><b>[output]</b> DataFrame with exactly the columns <code>code</code>, <code>uses</code>, <code>total_discount</code>, <code>capped_rides</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession, DataFrame, functions as F\n\n\ndef solve(spark: SparkSession, rides_df: DataFrame, promos_df: DataFrame) -> DataFrame:\n    # write your code here\n    return promos_df\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["join","left join","when / otherwise","least","groupBy / agg"],"minutes":15,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>solution.py</code>.</li><li>Complète la fonction <code>solve(spark, rides_df, promos_df)</code>. Garde son nom et ses paramètres : ce sont les tests qui l'appellent, avec leurs propres données. N'écris pas d'appel à la fonction toi-même.</li><li><code>spark</code> est une SparkSession déjà prête : ne crée pas la tienne.</li><li><code>rides_df</code>, <code>promos_df</code> sont des DataFrames déjà chargés : ses colonnes sont celles de l'énoncé (onglet <b>Input</b> d'un test pour les voir).</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Ta fonction doit <b>renvoyer</b> le résultat avec <code>return</code>. Un <code>print</code> ou un <code>show()</code> ne compte pas : il sort seulement dans l'onglet <b>Console Output</b>.</li><li>Le DataFrame renvoyé doit avoir <b>exactement 4 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>code</code>, <code>uses</code>, <code>total_discount</code>, <code>capped_rides</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Finis par un <code>select(...)</code> de ces colonnes.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>orderBy</code> à la fin).</li><li>Valeurs : les nombres sont comparés à 0,000001 près : arrondis seulement là où l'énoncé le demande, et au nombre de décimales demandé.</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Spark tourne dans ton Codespace : au premier RUN TESTS de la séance, attends qu'il soit prêt (1 à 2 minutes). Ensuite chaque lancement prend quelques secondes.</li><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 6 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"rides_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"ride_id STRING, ride_date DATE, fare DOUBLE, promo_code STRING","rows":[["R1","2026-03-02",40.0,"ramadan"],["R2","2026-03-10",80.0," RAMADAN "],["R3","2026-03-11",50.0,"RAMADAN"],["R4","2026-03-05",20.0,"Welcome"],["R5","2026-03-06",30.0,null],["R6","2026-03-07",60.0,"WELCOME10"]]}},"promos_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"code STRING, pct INT, max_discount DOUBLE, valid_from DATE, valid_to DATE","rows":[["RAMADAN",20,10.0,"2026-03-01","2026-03-10"],["WELCOME",50,15.0,"2026-03-01","2026-03-31"],["SPRING",10,5.0,"2026-03-20","2026-03-31"]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["code","uses","total_discount","capped_rides"],"rows":[["RAMADAN",2,18.0,1],["WELCOME",1,10.0,0],["SPRING",0,0.0,0]]}},{"input":{"rides_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"ride_id STRING, ride_date DATE, fare DOUBLE, promo_code STRING","rows":[["X","2026-03-31",100.0,"spring"],["Y","2026-03-20",50.0,"SPRING"]]}},"promos_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"code STRING, pct INT, max_discount DOUBLE, valid_from DATE, valid_to DATE","rows":[["RAMADAN",20,10.0,"2026-03-01","2026-03-10"],["WELCOME",50,15.0,"2026-03-01","2026-03-31"],["SPRING",10,5.0,"2026-03-20","2026-03-31"]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["code","uses","total_discount","capped_rides"],"rows":[["SPRING",2,10.0,1],["RAMADAN",0,0.0,0],["WELCOME",0,0.0,0]]}}],"hidden_n":6,"hidden":"","solution":""}